Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• "family_id": grouping key to identify a publication family (PUBLICATIONS)  
• "publication_date": to find the earliest publication in a family and keep only those first published in January 2015 (PUBLICATIONS)  
• "publication_number": later reported as a comma‑separated list (PUBLICATIONS)  
• "country_code": will be aggregated per family (PUBLICATIONS)  
• "cpc": variant column that stores one or more Cooperative Patent Classification symbols (PUBLICATIONS)  
• "ipc": variant column that stores one or more International Patent Classification symbols (PUBLICATIONS)  
• "citation": variant column that stores references to other patent publications (PUBLICATIONS) – used to discover families that are cited by / cite the focal family  
• "family_id" (again): after flattening "citation" we re‑join on PUBLICATIONS to translate publication numbers into their family_ids  

Typical conditions that will be used  
• WHERE "publication_date" BETWEEN 20150101 AND 20150131            – restrict to January 2015  
• QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY "family_id" ORDER BY "publication_date") = 1 – keep only the earliest publication of the family  
• LATERAL FLATTEN … ON "cpc", "ipc", or "citation" – explode VARIANT arrays/objects  
• DISTINCT and ARRAY_AGG(DISTINCT … ORDER BY …) – build comma‑separated lists in the final query  

Below are 10 exploratory Snowflake SQL queries (LIMIT 20 each) that incrementally examine the data and show how to arrive at the requested result.  
All column‑names are enclosed in double quotes and every query is preceded by a short description.

```sql
-- 1. Description: Peek at a few publications around January 2015 to confirm date format and available columns
SELECT 
  "publication_number",
  "publication_date",
  "family_id",
  "country_code",
  "cpc",
  "ipc",
  "citation"
FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS
WHERE "publication_date" BETWEEN 20150101 AND 20150131
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: Return the earliest publication (min date) per family for families that first appeared in Jan‑2015
SELECT
  "family_id",
  MIN("publication_date") AS "earliest_pub_date"
FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS
WHERE "publication_date" BETWEEN 20150101 AND 20150131
GROUP BY "family_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Produce one row per family showing its earliest publication in Jan‑2015 only
SELECT *
FROM (
  SELECT
    "family_id",
    "publication_number",
    "publication_date",
    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY "family_id" ORDER BY "publication_date") AS rn
  FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS
  WHERE "publication_date" BETWEEN 20150101 AND 20150131
)
WHERE rn = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: List all distinct publication_numbers belonging to the sample families discovered in query 3
SELECT DISTINCT
  p."family_id",
  p."publication_number"
FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS p
JOIN (
  SELECT DISTINCT "family_id"
  FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS
  WHERE "publication_date" BETWEEN 20150101 AND 20150131
) f
  ON p."family_id" = f."family_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: Flatten CPC codes for the same families (one CPC per row)
SELECT
  p."family_id",
  c.value::STRING AS "cpc_symbol"
FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS p,
     LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."cpc") c
WHERE p."family_id" IN (
  SELECT DISTINCT "family_id"
  FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS
  WHERE "publication_date" BETWEEN 20150101 AND 20150131
)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Flatten IPC codes for the same families (one IPC per row)
SELECT
  p."family_id",
  i.value::STRING AS "ipc_symbol"
FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS p,
     LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."ipc") i
WHERE p."family_id" IN (
  SELECT DISTINCT "family_id"
  FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS
  WHERE "publication_date" BETWEEN 20150101 AND 20150131
)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: Extract cited publication_numbers from the focal families’ publications
SELECT
  p."family_id"                 AS "source_family",
  c.value:"publication_number"::STRING  AS "cited_pub_number"
FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS p,
     LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."citation") c
WHERE p."family_id" IN (
  SELECT DISTINCT "family_id"
  FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS
  WHERE "publication_date" BETWEEN 20150101 AND 20150131
)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Map the cited publication_numbers to their family_ids (families that are cited BY the focal families)
SELECT DISTINCT
  src."source_family"      AS "citing_family",
  tgt."family_id"          AS "cited_family"
FROM (
  SELECT
    p."family_id" AS "source_family",
    c.value:"publication_number"::STRING AS "cited_pub_number"
  FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS p,
       LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."citation") c
  WHERE p."family_id" IN (
    SELECT DISTINCT "family_id"
    FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS
    WHERE "publication_date" BETWEEN 20150101 AND 20150131
  )
) src
JOIN PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS tgt
  ON tgt."publication_number" = src."cited_pub_number"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Families that CITE the focal families (reverse direction)
SELECT DISTINCT
  tgt."family_id"      AS "citing_family",
  src."family_id"      AS "cited_family"
FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS src
JOIN LATERAL FLATTEN(input => src."citation") c
  ON TRUE
JOIN PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS tgt
  ON tgt."publication_number" = c.value:"publication_number"::STRING
WHERE src."family_id" IN (
  SELECT DISTINCT "family_id"
  FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS
  WHERE "publication_date" BETWEEN 20150101 AND 20150131
)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: Aggregate everything into the final structure (one row per focal family)
SELECT
  f."family_id",
  MIN(p."publication_date")                                       AS "earliest_pub_date",
  ARRAY_TO_STRING(ARRAY_AGG(DISTINCT p."publication_number" ORDER BY p."publication_number"), ', ') AS "pub_numbers",
  ARRAY_TO_STRING(ARRAY_AGG(DISTINCT p."country_code" ORDER BY p."country_code"), ', ')            AS "country_codes",
  ARRAY_TO_STRING(ARRAY_AGG(DISTINCT cpc_sym ORDER BY cpc_sym), ', ')                              AS "cpc_codes",
  ARRAY_TO_STRING(ARRAY_AGG(DISTINCT ipc_sym ORDER BY ipc_sym), ', ')                              AS "ipc_codes",
  ARRAY_TO_STRING(ARRAY_AGG(DISTINCT cited_family ORDER BY cited_family), ', ')                    AS "cited_families",
  ARRAY_TO_STRING(ARRAY_AGG(DISTINCT citing_family ORDER BY citing_family), ', ')                  AS "citing_families"
FROM (
  -- families first published in Jan‑2015
  SELECT DISTINCT "family_id"
  FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS
  WHERE "publication_date" BETWEEN 20150101 AND 20150131
) f
LEFT JOIN PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS p
       ON p."family_id" = f."family_id"
LEFT JOIN LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."cpc") c1 ON TRUE
LEFT JOIN LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."ipc") i1 ON TRUE
LEFT JOIN (
  -- cited families
  SELECT DISTINCT
    src."family_id" AS source_family,
    tgt."family_id" AS cited_family
  FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS src,
       LATERAL FLATTEN(input => src."citation") cit
       JOIN PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS tgt
         ON tgt."publication_number" = cit.value:"publication_number"::STRING
) cf
  ON cf.source_family = f."family_id"
LEFT JOIN (
  -- citing families (reverse)
  SELECT DISTINCT
    tgt."family_id" AS citing_family,
    src."family_id" AS target_family
  FROM PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS src,
       LATERAL FLATTEN(input => src."citation") cit
       JOIN PATENTS_GOOGLE.PATENTS_GOOGLE.PUBLICATIONS tgt
         ON tgt."publication_number" = cit.value:"publication_number"::STRING
) rf
  ON rf.target_family = f."family_id"
GROUP BY f."family_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

The ten queries go from simple inspection (queries 1–3) through progressively richer extractions (queries 4–9) and culminate in query 10, which produces exactly the columns the task asks for, ready for further formatting.
